enchilada: GL: CoreAnimation's display surfaces drawn by the GPU in place, kernel-maintained

With 0198 the GPU never touched a cacheable IOSurface: eagl.c rendered each 1125x2436 frame into a
texture of its own and glReadPixels'd it into the surface (~72 ms a frame, gl-run-20), and refilled
a display-sized source texture from its surface (10809 KB, ~20 ms a frame). Those surfaces are
wired IOBufferMemoryDescriptor pages with a cacheable kernel alias, so they can be kept coherent
like 0197's arena BOs, by kernel address instead of a pa: dc civac by VA reaches every alias of a
page (the data cache is physically tagged). SURFACE_BO now maps such a surface (one plane, not
client memory) and marks the BO zero-copy; SUBMIT cleans its pixels before the GPU runs and
records the fence on the surface (gpuDirty, gpuFence: the surface, not the BO, since the panel
copy and other tasks see the surface); CPU_PREP, WAIT_FENCE and BO_SYNC drop its lines once the GPU
is done; CPU_FINI and BO_SYNC clean. The panel copy and the IOSurface lock trap call
sweet_surface_gpu_settle(): if the GPU used the surface since its lines were last dropped, wait
(at most 100 ms, then read it as it is) for that submission and clean+invalidate, so it is done
once per GPU use and costs nothing (no lock) otherwise. clientMemoryForType() gives such a BO
copyback: left to the libdrm stand-in's write-combined request, mapping it would re-type the
surface's VM object, the panel copy's alias with it. Client memory (wrapped task ranges) and planar
surfaces are still refused, the client copies them. One line per kind of surface says which path
it took; boot-arg glzc0=1 refuses every cacheable surface again (0198's behaviour; the client copies).

